\f2\fs28 \cf4 Altimeter Serial Telemetry Protocol
\f1\fs22 \cf3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa360\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs20 \cf5 April 2026  |  Revision 1.1
\f1\fs22 \cf3 \
\pard\sa360\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs32 \cf2 1. Purpose\
\pard\sa360\partightenfactor0

\fs22 \cf3 This document specifies the serial telemetry interface between the altimeter board and the LoRa tracker board. It is the single reference for the $PYRO NMEA protocol: the state numbering scheme, the event sentences, and the flags bitfield. Any Claude session working on the altimeter firmware should read this document before making changes to the telemetry output.
\fs32 \cf2 \

\fs22 \cf3 The tracker firmware, the Data Source Manager (DSM), and the ground station web application all depend on this interface. Changes to the altimeter must preserve compatibility with these downstream consumers.
\fs32 \cf2 \
2. Background: The State Mismatch Problem\

\fs22 \cf3 During a pressure chamber test (flight 4), the ground station displayed incorrect flight phase labels and then triggered a false signal-loss alert while the tracker was still transmitting normally. Root cause analysis traced the problem to a mismatch between the flight state numbers the altimeter was transmitting and the numbers the ground station expects.
\fs32 \cf2 \

\fs22 \cf3 The ground station uses a 5-state scheme (codes 0 through 4). The altimeter was transmitting a different set of state codes. When the altimeter entered its descent phase, the ground station interpreted the state code as LANDED. Unknown state codes for subsequent phases caused the display to show no recognized state, which the health monitoring interpreted as signal loss.
\fs32 \cf2 \

\fs22 \cf3 The fix: The altimeter must transmit the state_id values defined in Section 3 below, using the thrust flag to differentiate boost from coast. This is a change to the 
\f3\fs20 state_to_telem_id()
\f0\fs22  mapping function, not to the altimeter's internal state machine.
\fs32 \cf2 \
3. Architecture: Two-Layer State Model\

\fs22 \cf3 This is the most important concept in this document. The altimeter has two independent layers for flight states, and they must not be confused:
\fs32 \cf2 \

\fs22 \cf3 Layer 1 \'97 Internal State Machine: The altimeter's own flight logic. This can have any number of states \'97 boot sequences, calibration phases, apogee detection, separate drogue and main descent, error recovery, etc. This is the altimeter developer's domain and the ground station has no opinion on it. The more granular it is, the better the altimeter's flight logic can be.
\fs32 \cf2 \

\fs22 \cf3 Layer 2 \'97 Reported Telemetry State (state_id): The single integer in the $PYRO sentence (field 2) that the tracker, DSM, and ground station all parse. This is a fixed contract with exactly 4 valid values (0, 1, 2, 3). These values must not change \'97 they are baked into the tracker firmware, the DSM parsers, and the ground station.
\fs32 \cf2 \
\pard\sa240\partightenfactor0

\fs22 \cf3 The mapping function 
\f3\fs20 state_to_telem_id()
\f0\fs22  is the bridge between these two layers. It takes any internal state and returns one of the 4 telemetry codes. Every internal state must have a mapping. Any unmapped state that leaks to the serial output will break the ground station.
\fs32 \cf2 \
\pard\sa240\partightenfactor0

