\fs22 \cf3 Key takeaway: The number of internal states can grow freely \'97 add STAGING, BALLISTIC_DESCENT, ERROR_RECOVERY, or whatever the flight logic needs. The only constraint is that 
\f3\fs20 state_to_telem_id()
\f0\fs22  must map every internal state to one of the 4 telemetry codes (0, 1, 2, 3). The ground station never sees the internal states; it only sees what comes out of this mapping function.
\fs26 \cf2 \
\pard\sa360\partightenfactor0

\fs22 \cf3 What went wrong in flight 4: The altimeter transmitted its raw internal state codes directly on serial, bypassing the mapping. Internal states beyond the original four appeared as unrecognized numbers on the ground station, causing incorrect display and broken lifecycle logic.
\fs26 \cf2 \
\pard\sa360\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 4. Telemetry State Codes (the Fixed Contract)\
\pard\sa360\partightenfactor0

\fs22 \cf3 The $PYRO sentence state field (field 2) must use these exact integer codes. The ground station, the DSM parsers, and the tracker firmware all key on these numbers. These 4 codes are the output of the mapping function described in Section 3.
